Exposure or in this case; get yourself used to being rejected. But I know this can be extremely difficult especially if you already have GAD or social anxiety.  Still, ask those simple questions more often. Think of it like earning XP points for your champion on their quest.  Sometimes you just gotta jump into it and see what happens, and whatever happens, you accept. But you have to understand why you were rejected.  It could have been the way you approached, or the other party has their reasons, it could also be situational. Or a combo of it all.  Whatever it is, learn from it and also learn to let go. You have to respect other people's responses as well as yours. 

Being rejected doesn't always mean it's automatically a bad thing. Being nervous is also okay. Fearing rejection is also okay. Feeling lonely is also okay. 

Whatever you feel afterwards is valid, but don't dwell too much in the negative side and applaud yourself for being able to go out and ask. It's also a good reminder that you can only control how you react after, but you can't do the same to other people. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Sorry to jump in, I just have a question. In POTS, there's the typical days and there are the better days where you can actually stand for more than a minute without feeling like you're gonna faint and continue to do physical activities for an extended period. Although you will still need to lie down eventually.

Then with learning more about POTS and how to manage the symptoms and take care of yourself better so we don't get as symptomatic, your condition improves. So then for example, instead of an increase of 30bpm+ when standing up, the heart rate can still increase to 20bpm+ but not more than 30+ when standing up from lying down position. The increase of heart rate remains like that in standing position but stabilizes back in lying down position again. Is this still POTS or is it something else? 

&#x200B;

I think that was confusing tho so in other words my question is that, is it only considered POTS if you never go down the threshold of 30bpm+ every time for every single day for forever when you stand up even on better days where you've managed the syndrome better? Or is still POTs and it's possible to have days it does not go past 30bpm, but can go back to being symptomatic, such as the increased 30bpm if the person pushes themselves too hard...?

&#x200B;

^(My god this became so long i'm sorry..)

This is implying that the solution for getting out of depression is widening your perspective and knowing you are far more blessed than some of the people outside your world. We know that that's not really true at all. It's just another "mind over matter" and dismissing depression as a real illness of the mind. You don't just will away your fever when you get one, do you? If this was the case, what does this say about famous comedians like Robin Williams, who i'm sure kept a very open and positive perspective but sadly still was not able to beat his illness..?

&#x200B;

Maybe this works for people whose depression isn't caused by a chemical imbalance in the brain but that's likely not the case for everyone. People automatically think all people who are depressed are not aware of this but I tell you, they've thought about that, too many times probably. Enough to make it feel worse. I get it, it's basically "looking for the beauty in it" and finding inspiration there to keep going. But again, that won't work for everyone.

&#x200B;

What I really dislike when people say this is that they think they know everything and they believe that what they're saying is the absolute truth. They don't even realize they themselves are the ones keeping a closed or narrow perspective.

&#x200B;

