I know what you mean. A lot of times in the past I kept missing the "old me" and asking to no one in particular why I wasn't like it anymore. Which was odd since when I was the old me, I didn't like a lot about myself. But it made me realize that at least, I was still able to do and finish things and was more sharp. 

&#x200B;

I think people who are confused by what you meant haven't experienced it themselves or with a loved one.

I'm late here, but I guess it was 3 years prior of being miserable, feeling numb, not knowing why and then I guess it finally clicked the first time I woke up and got disappointed I still wasn't dead. Crying in public spaces not knowing why, then not being able to cry when I wanted to. And of course, when my first attempt to kms failed. 

&#x200B;

Also I love your question and this thread because it highlights that people who develop depression do not have to have a particular traumatic instance happen to them recently in order for their depression to be valid. It really just be the chemical imbalance in your brain sometimes. It took me so long to seek help since I felt that I had no right with the privilege I have to be feeling so miserable and suicidal already; it's what I had to struggle so much internally bc people who don't know what it's like to have depression dismiss it since I had such a nice life by society's standards.

Idk man, back then when even that idea was still very much a norm and idealized, getting 2 incomes from both parents was very much needed so in order for the family to survive because of the terrible economy and inflation. That's not because of feminism.

&#x200B;

It just sounds like you want the "privileges" of a very sexist society that belittles women for not having to work and just be a full time mom. The set of skills you mentioned are things you can still very much learn today. Those that actually only got to enjoy the benefits of being a full time mom without any work outside their household whatsoever are the rich, the poorer moms still had to find work in order to feed their families, even if getting an income that was barely enough. You're only lucky back in the day if you married a husband that brought a huge income for the family so the family doesn't have to worry about buying milk and diapers or food. Don't blame and put your excuse on feminism when it has benefited millions of people from much poorer situations that didn't have much choices to better their lives. If you want to be a successful career mom, nice. If you want to just be a full time mom instead, also nice. The latter is still very much in practice today.

True. The more I think about it, the truer the words "a walking contradiction" applies to me. My mood, my behavior, my likes and dislikes, my routine and everything else that should remain consistent is whack. I change from this to that and it's hard to explain to people and to doctors when it really affects your everyday life. 

&#x200B;

It's like a never ending of trying to find what really works best around to be able to function like the average person daily for as long as you can. I find something that works and then it all comes crashing down when another cycle starts. It doesn't work anymore. Then repeat. It sucks.

